Prove the remote ledger refines a serial prefix (@computing-protocol-invariant: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Preserve-operation-identity-across-crashes-and-old-message-lifetimes.lingua#r_00F9BB17D72DBC9B162FFDFC4E") { r_00DCF55733BDF283803289BC1C
Let the fixed jobs be x₁,…,x_M and define P₀=0 and P_j=Σᵢ₌₁ʲxᵢ. Let c be the number of client-confirmed results and r the server's committed identity. The key invariant is 0≤c≤r≤min(c+1,M). The server tuple is (r,P_r,x_r) for r>0 and (0,0,0) initially. Its actual effect history is exactly ((1,x₁),…,(r,x_r)); client results are (P₁,…,P_c). Thus the server may be one operation ahead of the client's knowledge, but no accepted result precedes its committed effect.

Also maintain message provenance. Every queued request (i,x) has 1≤i≤min(c+1,M) and x=x_i. Every queued reply (i,v) has 1≤i≤r and v=P_i. Old messages can remain indefinitely; the invariant does not require clearing either channel. Its effect-history variable is a mathematical witness and an explorer field, not a second unbounded log that the implementation needs for duplicate recognition.

Prove preservation one event at a time. Sending inserts only the current request, so provenance holds. Dropping removes a message and changes no endpoint state. Delivering a stale request changes nothing. Delivering a duplicate with i=r returns the same P_r and appends no effect. For a new accepted request, i=r+1; provenance gives i≤c+1, while r≥c, forcing r=c and i=c+1. The atomic addition extends exactly the next serial prefix, so r becomes c+1 and its reply has the correct P_r.

An accepted reply has i=c+1 and provenance gives i≤r. Combined with r≤c+1, this forces r=c+1. Advancing c makes c=r and appends the correct next prefix sum. An old reply changes nothing. A crash between atomic durable events preserves endpoint tuples; the model permits channel state to survive, so old-message safety remains necessary. Initialization satisfies every clause. Induction therefore proves the invariant for every finite execution of any representable declared workload, not only the explored workloads.

Erase sends, drops, duplicates, ignored replies and crashes from an execution, retaining just successful server commits. The remaining operation sequence is a prefix of the serial specification; accepted client results correspond to its same prefix sums. This is a refinement argument. The observable client history may stop before the server's history. A timeout reported as unknown must not be relabeled as a proof of no effect. Multi-client real-time ordering and a general linearizability proof are beyond this single-client example.

For M jobs, the reachable set has exactly 3·4^M−2 states in this chosen abstraction. At confirmed prefix c<M, r is c or c+1. Requests can be any subset of the first c+1 identities, and replies any subset of the first r. This gives 2^(c+1+c)+2^(c+1+c+1)=6·4^c states. At c=r=M there are 4^M channel-subset choices. Summing the geometric series gives the formula, also one state for M=0. To establish attainability rather than merely an upper bound, retain all messages while completing a prefix, optionally commit the next request, then drop whichever retained messages the desired subsets omit. At r=c, send the next request only if desired. This constructs every counted state.

**Work:** derive counts 1,10,46,190 for M=0,1,2,3. Reuse [Review discrete arguments counting and preserved structure](../../mathematics/core/Review-discrete-arguments-counting-and-preserved-structure.lingua#r_002045D0F1E9276EAC8563DADF) for subset counting and graph reachability and [Investigate concurrent updates waiting and independent state evidence](Investigate-concurrent-updates-waiting-and-independent-state-evidence.lingua#r_0003B96BB09E60CBDBD0789C4F) for state exploration versus proofs. The independent abstract checker uses only prefix lengths and sets of identities, reconstructing numeric totals separately. Explain why a bijection of states and equal projected transitions supplies stronger finite evidence than checking a final total alone.

